<.guide_page
  signed_in?={@signed_in?}
  title={@guide_meta.title}
  dek={@guide_meta.dek}
  date={@guide_meta.date}
  read_time={@guide_meta.read_time}
>
  <p>
    We want our agents to have eyes on production. If an order is stuck, we’d like to ask
    “what’s holding up checkout?” and let the agent look. We don’t want to copy logs into chat
    or guess which database query it should run.
  </p>
  <p>
    The catch is what happens when we connect all the tools we use. Some MCP clients send
    the model every tool’s description and arguments before it can make its first call.
    Now a question about a stuck order starts with the agent reading how to check the memory
    usage of a Redis key. We still want Redis available for the next incident.
  </p>
  <p>
    We built Emisar with 14 tools. One of them, <code>find_actions</code>, lets the agent
    look up what it can do on the hosts it has access to. When we added the ability to open
    a Sentry event by ID, it became searchable through that same tool. Nobody had to install
    another connector, and the tool list stayed the same size.
  </p>

  <h2 id="token-count">What goes into the first prompt?</h2>
  <p>
    We used the November 24, 2026 catalog to model what a client would load if each action
    were its own MCP tool. We counted the name, description and full argument schema for each
    action, then used the same tokenizer on Emisar’s actual <code>tools/list</code> response.
  </p>
  <figure class="my-8">
    <figcaption class="mb-4 text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">
      Tokens in tool definitions before the first call
    </figcaption>
    <table class="w-full text-left text-sm leading-5 text-zinc-300">
      <thead class="border-b border-zinc-700">
        <tr>
          <th scope="col" class="pb-3 pr-3 font-medium">Available actions</th>
          <th scope="col" class="px-2 pb-3 text-right font-medium">One tool per action</th>
          <th scope="col" class="pb-3 pl-2 text-right font-medium text-brand-300">emisar</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-800 tabular-nums">
        <tr :for={
          {scope, actions, eager} <- [
            {"PostgreSQL", "49 actions", "7,158"},
            {"PostgreSQL, Kubernetes, Redis, Sentry", "167 actions", "32,015"},
            {"Full catalog", "1,918 actions across 108 packs", "391,423"}
          ]
        }>
          <th scope="row" class="py-3.5 pr-3 font-normal">
            <span class="block text-zinc-100">{scope}</span>
            <span class="text-xs text-zinc-400">{actions}</span>
          </th>
          <td class="px-2 text-right">{eager}</td>
          <td class="pl-2 text-right font-semibold text-brand-300">10,572</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </figure>
  <p>
    If PostgreSQL is all you need, its 49 tools are cheaper to load directly. Emisar uses
    3,414 more tokens up front. Two runbook tools alone use 4,582 tokens to explain how to create and edit a procedure.
    You pay that cost even if all you want is one database read.
  </p>
  <p>
    Add Kubernetes, Redis and Sentry, and the arithmetic flips. Loading those four packs
    up front takes 32,015 tokens. Emisar still takes 10,572. Across the full catalog that’s <strong>97.3% fewer tokens in the initial tool definitions</strong>. If your client already
    loads tools on demand, check what it actually sends the model. Anthropic shows how much
    that choice matters in its <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/advanced-tool-use">five-server example</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="discovery">Which session is holding up order 42?</h2>
  <p>
    In our test, one PostgreSQL session marked order 42 as paid. Another tried to mark
    it sent and waited on a lock. We wanted to know how much the agent would have to read
    to find the session holding it. We searched a local workspace with all 108 packs
    across 15 test runners. You can make the same search from the CLI.
  </p>
  <.docs_code
    phx-no-format
    label="Find the lock query"
    copy_text={"emisar-mcp find_actions '{\n  \"query\": \"blocked queries waiting on locks\",\n  \"limit\": 3\n}' --json > matches.json\njq -r '.candidates[].action_id' matches.json"}
  >emisar-mcp find_actions '&#123;
  "query": "blocked queries waiting on locks",
  "limit": 3
}' --json &gt; matches.json
jq -r '.candidates[].action_id' matches.json

postgres.lock_blocking_chains
gcp.sql_query_insights
mysql.lock_waits</.docs_code>
  <p>
    Another search, “which postgres session is holding up an update,” missed the right
    action in the top three. “Blocked queries waiting on locks” found it. It also found
    a MySQL action. If you already know which database you’re looking at, narrow the
    search with <code>pack_id</code> set to <code>postgres</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each match includes a <code>next</code>
    call with the action ID and the exact pack hash. Pass those arguments to
    <code>get_action</code>
    to see what the action needs and which host can run it.
  </p>
  <.docs_code
    phx-no-format
    label="Check the arguments and host"
    copy_text={"jq '.candidates[] | select(.action_id == \"postgres.lock_blocking_chains\")\n    | .next.arguments' matches.json |\n  emisar-mcp get_action - --json > action.json\njq '{risk: .action.risk, args: .action.args_schema.properties,\n    targets: [.compatible_runners[].name]}' action.json"}
  >jq '.candidates[] | select(.action_id == "postgres.lock_blocking_chains")
    | .next.arguments' matches.json |
  emisar-mcp get_action - --json &gt; action.json
jq '&#123;risk: .action.risk, args: .action.args_schema.properties,
    targets: [.compatible_runners[].name]}' action.json

&#123;
  "risk": "medium",
  "args": &#123;},
  "targets": ["pg-primary"]
}</.docs_code>
  <figure class="my-8 border-y border-zinc-800 py-6" aria-labelledby="lookup-cost">
    <figcaption id="lookup-cost" class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
      12,145 tokens before the first database read
    </figcaption>
    <div
      aria-hidden="true"
      class="mt-5 grid h-3 grid-cols-[10572fr_1242fr_331fr] gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-sm"
    >
      <span class="bg-zinc-500"></span>
      <span class="bg-brand-500"></span>
      <span class="bg-brand-200"></span>
    </div>
    <dl class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-3 gap-3 text-sm">
      <div>
        <dt class="text-zinc-400">Fixed tool list</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 font-mono text-zinc-100">10,572</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="text-zinc-400">Search result</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 font-mono text-brand-400">1,242</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="text-zinc-400">Action detail</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 font-mono text-brand-200">331</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </figure>
  <p>
    We counted the full search result and action details once each, before trimming them
    for display. A client that sends both the structured result and its text copy spends
    more. Tool-call inputs, conversation history and the eventual database output cost
    tokens too.
  </p>
  <p>
    The agent now has enough to request <code>postgres.lock_blocking_chains</code>
    on <code>pg-primary</code>. It supplies the pack and runner references, an empty argument
    object and a reason. Even this read is rated medium risk because SQL text can contain
    customer data. The control plane applies the team’s approval policy, and the runner
    checks the action and its arguments on the host.
  </p>
  <p>
    What would that tell us during an incident? In a separate PostgreSQL 17.12 test, we ran
    the pack’s queries with a monitoring role. The first read identified both sessions.
    A second, <code>postgres.activity_detail</code>, told us what the one holding the lock
    was doing.
  </p>
  <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Order 42 · selected fields">Waiting   198   UPDATE orders SET state = 'sent' WHERE id = 42;
Holding   186   UPDATE orders SET state = 'paid' WHERE id = 42;
State     186   idle in transaction</.docs_code>
  <p>
    Session 186 had finished the update and left its transaction open. There was no running
    query to cancel. We rolled back the transaction from session 186 in the test. The waiting
    update finished, order 42 was marked sent and the lock query came back empty. You can <.link href={
      ~p"/packs/postgres#postgres-ai-example"
    }>try this in three psql sessions</.link>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="execution">Who gets to end the database session?</h2>
  <p>
    Cloudflare took another approach to a large catalog. Its agent writes JavaScript to
    search an API specification and call the API in a sandbox. That’s useful when a question
    needs several requests and you only want a few fields back.
    <a href="https://blog.cloudflare.com/code-mode-mcp/">Cloudflare’s ruleset example</a>
    shows how it works.
  </p>
  <p>
    On our database, we want the agent to find out who holds a lock without asking us.
    If it wants to terminate that session, we want to hear why. An approval for “run SQL”
    is too broad to express that. Emisar gives those jobs separate action IDs. The pack
    defines the query and which arguments the agent can supply. We can approve the read in
    advance and have the control plane hold the termination for review, whether the request
    comes from a teammate’s terminal or our Slack bot.
  </p>
  <p>
    The cost of that choice is that someone has to define each action. If the one you need
    is missing, add it to a pack, install it on the relevant hosts and trust that version.
    The agent can then find it with the tools it already has.
  </p>

  <h2 id="measure">Measure your own MCP setup</h2>
  <p>
    Save the <code>tools</code>
    arrays from every page of your servers’ <code>tools/list</code>
    responses and combine them into one JSON array. For Emisar, sign in and run
    <code>emisar-mcp list_tools --json</code>
    to get the array. The script below counts
    tokens with <code>o200k_base</code>. If your client rewrites tool descriptions or loads
    them on demand, also count the definitions in the first request it sends the model.
  </p>
  <.docs_code phx-no-format label="count_tools.py" copy_id="count-tools-python">import json
import sys
import tiktoken

with open(sys.argv[1], encoding="utf-8") as source:
    tools = json.load(source)
text = json.dumps(tools, ensure_ascii=False,
                  sort_keys=True, separators=(",", ":"))
tokens = tiktoken.get_encoding("o200k_base").encode_ordinary(text)
print(f"&#123;len(tools):,} tools, &#123;len(tokens):,} tokens")</.docs_code>
  <details class="my-6 rounded-xl border border-zinc-800 px-5 py-4">
    <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-zinc-200">
      Reproduce the three catalog comparisons
    </summary>
    <p>
      We used the
      <a href="https://github.com/andrewdryga/emisar/tree/21ae3e38a6a58b32844b42d0388b218ba471eb5e">November 24 source snapshot</a>
      and Python’s <code>tiktoken</code>
      0.14.0. In a configured checkout of that revision,
      save this file in <code>portal/</code>
      and run <code>mix run --no-start snapshot.exs</code>
      there. It writes one JSON array per row and the fixed MCP list. Pass each file to the
      counter above. Running <code>python count_tools.py stack.json</code>
      prints <code>167 tools, 32,015 tokens</code>.
    </p>
    <.docs_code phx-no-format label="snapshot.exs" copy_id="snapshot-elixir">alias EmisarWeb.MCP.&#123;SchemaRegistry, ToolSchema}

packs = Jason.decode!(File.read!("apps/emisar/priv/packs/catalog.json"))["packs"]
for &#123;file, ids} &lt;- [&#123;"postgres", ["postgres"]},
                    &#123;"stack", ~w(postgres kubernetes redis sentry)},
                    &#123;"catalog", :all}] do
  tools = for pack &lt;- packs, ids == :all or pack["id"] in ids,
              action &lt;- pack["actions"] do
    schema = ToolSchema.action_args_schema(%&#123;args_schema: %&#123;"args" =&gt; action["args"] || []}})
    %&#123;name: action["id"], description: action["description"], inputSchema: schema}
  end
  File.write!(file &lt;&gt; ".json", Jason.encode!(tools))
end
File.write!("emisar.json", Jason.encode!(SchemaRegistry.tools()))</.docs_code>
  </details>
  <p>
    For a question you ask regularly, try both setups in fresh sessions with the same
    model. Compare the first request, then add up the input tokens through the answer.
    If the agent needs three searches or returns a whole table, you’ll see what that cost too.
  </p>
</.guide_page>
